From 8daddcfc340c0a6cd34f87b4f7e1b331fee3aa37 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 08:58:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Simplify the site, fix sign-in, and make paying
 straightforward
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A single change set covering catalog clean-up, the broken login, the
payment flow, and interface polish.

DUPLICATE PETS
- The home page listed the same pet in both "Featured" and "Rare Breeds
  VIP". Replaced the overlapping sections with one grid of unique pets.
- The catalog de-duplicates by pet id, and the admin "add pet" action
  refuses an id that is already listed.

SIGN-IN AND ACCOUNT CREATION
- Login was gated behind a 6-digit code emailed via Resend. Without a
  Resend key the server reported success and sent nothing, leaving people
  stuck on a code screen forever; codes also lived in memory and died on
  restart. The gate is gone — Firebase Auth verifies the password, which
  is what actually secured the account.
- Firebase is now the single source of truth for the session, replacing a
  localStorage copy that could claim you were signed in after the real
  session had expired, racing with onAuthStateChanged.
- New visitors were shown a saved email of eleanor.vance@beverlyhills.org
  and invited to sign in as her. Removed.
- Firebase error codes are translated into plain English.
- Gated pages wait for Firebase before deciding you are signed out.

PAYING, WITH WHATSAPP AS THE CONFIRMATION STEP
- Checkout is one page: your details, destination, payment preference.
- Reserving opens WhatsApp with the order details pre-filled.
- Every surface states that nothing is charged on the website and that
  confirmation and shipment are arranged on WhatsApp.
- Buyers now receive their own confirmation email explaining the next
  step; previously only the store owner was emailed.
- Removed the Apple gift-card field, which granted $100 off for any code
  of eight or more characters with no validation.
- FAQs rewritten around how payment and shipment actually work.

FEWER CLICKS
- "Reserve" means one thing everywhere: into the basket and straight to
  the reservation form. Any pet is browse -> card -> reserve.
- Every card offers both Details and Reserve, not one or the other.

REMOVED WHAT WAS UNUSED OR UNREACHABLE
- The compare feature had no way to be opened at all.
- The language switcher translated nothing.
- Recently-viewed and selected-breeder state was recorded, never shown.
- Home page trimmed from eight sections to five; the removed ones
  duplicated pages already in the navigation.
- Footer collection links now apply the filter they name instead of five
  links to the same page; dropped the legal row that repeated other links
  and pointed Privacy and Terms at the FAQ.
- Browse: one photo-scan button instead of two, and the personality
  filter shows the twelve most common traits rather than all thirty.
- Header nav renders from one list; the Admin shortcut shows only to
  admins, where before it was visible to every customer.

LOOK AND FEEL
- animate-fade-in was used in 21 files but never defined, so nothing
  animated. Defined it, plus a staggered entrance for card grids.
- The web fonts were imported in a way the build dropped, so the site
  silently fell back to Georgia and system sans. Loaded from index.html
  with preconnect; Playfair and Inter now render.
- Visible focus rings for keyboard users, and prefers-reduced-motion is
  honoured throughout.

PET PHOTOS
- Real photos now front the catalog: 42 supplied images across all 17
  breeds, renamed from their upload names to the breed they show
  (Austrelian sherperd.jpg -> australian-shepherd.jpg, Main coon 2.jpg ->
  maine-coon-2.jpg, and so on) so each listing picks up its own.
- Photos dropped into src/assets/images/ are picked up automatically: name
  a file after the breed and every pet of that breed uses it; name it after
  a pet id (pet-1.jpg) for one animal only. Extra angles become gallery
  thumbnails. Capitals and spaces in file names are tolerated.
- The home banner and category tiles use these photos instead of stock
  images, and the banner shows them at their natural size rather than
  stretching a 450px photo across the full width.
- Photos never break: a missing or failed image shows a branded
  placeholder carrying the breed name, and breeder and reviewer portraits
  fall back to their initials, instead of a broken-image icon.
- The gallery thumbnail strip only appears when there is more than one
  photo, and resets to the first photo when you move to a different pet.
- File names are all lower case, and no rename changes capitalisation alone.
  Windows and macOS treat Samoyed.jpg and samoyed.jpg as the same file, so
  such a rename cannot be applied there; the first Samoyed photo is
  therefore samoyed-1.jpg, which the matcher treats the same way.
- The home page looks its photos up by breed rather than importing a file
  path, so renaming or replacing a photo cannot break the build.
- A photo supplied as "Golden retriever 3" is a black Labrador-type puppy,
  not a Golden Retriever. It is kept as unassigned-black-labrador-puppy.jpg
  and shown nowhere, rather than published under the wrong breed. Anything
  named unassigned-* is held back the same way.

PET NAMES REMOVED FROM DISPLAY
- Cards, the pet page, quick view, cart, order tracking and the customer
  dashboard now lead with the breed. Where a card read "Bella - 2 Months
  Old - Female" it now reads "2 Months Old - Female - Honey Cream".
- Buttons and headings follow: "Reserve this puppy now", "Ask about this
  Golden Retriever on WhatsApp".
- Pre-filled WhatsApp messages and order records identify a pet by breed
  and listing id instead of by name, so you can still tell which animal an
  order refers to.
- Testimonials were rewritten to praise the breed rather than name a pet.
- Names are kept in the data and in the admin inventory table, which only
  you see, so you can still identify individual animals.

EMAILJS ORDER NOTIFICATIONS
- Every placed order is emailed to the store owner through EmailJS, built
  from the real order returned by placeOrder — no sample or test data.
- Order of operations: the order is recorded first, and only if that
  succeeds is the email sent; WhatsApp opens last.
- The customer sees a spinner while it sends, a confirmation when it
  lands, and, if the send fails, a note that the reservation is safe with
  a prompt to quote the order number on WhatsApp.
- Duplicate protection on two levels: a ref blocks a second submit before
  React state can catch up, and each order id is emailed at most once. A
  genuine failure releases the id so the customer can retry.
- The $50 deposit hold follows the same sequence.
- Service, template and public key come from VITE_EMAILJS_* with the
  account's values as defaults. These are EmailJS's public browser
  identifiers, not secrets; the Private Key is not used anywhere.
- docs/emailjs-template.md lists the exact variable names the template
  must use and a body to paste in.

REGISTRATION ERRORS AND FIREBASE CONFIGURATION
- Every Firebase auth error now has its own message. An existing email, a
  weak password, a wrong password and a disabled provider previously
  collapsed into the same line because sign-up was rejected at the
  provider before any of the others could be reached.
- Codes are matched by prefix as well as exactly. Firebase appends server
  text to some codes — a rejected key arrives as
  "auth/api-key-not-valid.-please-pass-a-valid-api-key." — which an exact
  lookup missed, leaking a raw FirebaseError string to the customer.
- Faults only the site owner can fix (provider disabled, sign-up
  restricted, unauthorised domain, bad or missing key, Authentication not
  set up) print the exact remedy to the console, naming the project, while
  the customer sees a plain sentence.
- Firebase settings can now come from VITE_FIREBASE_* in .env, falling
  back to the bundled firebase-applet-config.json, so the site can be
  pointed at a different project without editing a committed file. All
  three of key, project id and app id are required before the override
  applies, so a half-filled .env cannot silently half-work.
- A blank or placeholder key is reported at startup instead of failing
  later with an opaque error.
- On sign-up the auth listener could overwrite the name just entered with
  the part of the email before the @, because it can fire before the
  display name has been stored. The better name is now kept.
- docs/firebase-setup.md records what is switched off on the bundled
  project and the exact console steps, for either owning that project or
  moving to your own.

OTHER FIXES
- The photo breed scanner invented a Golden Retriever result at 94%
  confidence and a $2,400 price whenever the API call failed. It now
  reports the failure and offers WhatsApp. Uploads capped at 6 MB.
- The AI matchmaker opened an unrelated pet when it recommended a breed
  not in stock; it now searches for the nearest match.
- Order tracking claimed payment was confirmed and the vet check done the
  moment an order was placed. The timeline now reflects reality.
- The newsletter promised a $150 voucher that nothing ever sends.
- Reserve button advertised a $300 deposit; the form charges $50.
- The cart charged $150 shipping against checkout's $100 domestic rate.
- The pet page labelled price-with-add-ons as the listing price.
- The footer logo pointed at a dev-only /src path that 404s in
  production; header and footer now share one vector mark, dropping a
  540 KB image from the bundle.
- Admin "average listing value" was hardcoded; it is now calculated.
- Checkout no longer shows buyers the store owner's private inbox.
- WhatsApp contact details live in one module instead of nine files.
- Page title was still "My Google AI Studio App".
- README dropped the Python virtualenv steps this Node app never uses.

